Handling land encroachment in India (specifically Telangana) involves a mix of revenue, police, and court remedies, depending on whether the land is private, government, or GP (Gram Panchayat) land.

Below is a clear, practical step-by-step guide 👇

1️⃣ First: Confirm Ownership & Encroachment

Before taking action, ensure your documents are clear.

Collect these documents:

Registered Sale Deed

Link documents

EC (Encumbrance Certificate)

Pattadar Passbook / Title Deed

Survey Number & Boundaries

Layout approval (if any)

Adangal / Pahani (for agricultural land)

➡️ If boundaries are unclear, get a survey done.

2️⃣ Apply for Official Land Survey (Very Important)

Encroachment cases are strongest after a government survey.

How to do it in Telangana:

Apply at MRO / Tahsildar office

Request Survey & Demarcation

Pay prescribed fee

Surveyor will measure land and fix boundary stones

📌 Survey report is key legal evidence.

3️⃣ Issue a Legal Notice

If encroachment is confirmed:

Send a legal notice through an advocate

Mention:

Survey findings

Land details

Time (usually 15–30 days) to vacate

Often, disputes get resolved at this stage.

4️⃣ File Complaint with Revenue Authorities

Depending on land type:

For private land encroachment:

File complaint to:

Tahsildar

Revenue Divisional Officer (RDO)

For Government / GP land:

Complaint to:

Tahsildar

District Collector

Gram Panchayat Secretary

Authorities can issue eviction notices under revenue laws.

5️⃣ Police Complaint (Limited but Useful)

Police do not decide ownership, but they help if:

There is forcible occupation

Threats or violence

Damage to fencing / crops

File complaint under:

Criminal Trespass – IPC 441

Mischief / Intimidation (if applicable)

6️⃣ File Civil Case (Strongest Remedy)

If the encroacher does not vacate:

File a case in Civil Court:

Suit for Declaration of Title

Suit for Permanent Injunction

Suit for Mandatory Injunction (Removal of encroachment)

Court may grant:

Temporary injunction

Stay order

Demolition / eviction order

⏳ This is slower but legally final.

7️⃣ If Encroachment Is by Government / Road / Drain

File representation to District Collector

If ignored → Writ Petition in High Court (Article 226)

Courts act faster in such cases.

8️⃣ Important Telangana Laws Used

Telangana Land Encroachment Act, 1905

TS Rights in Land & Pattadar Passbooks Act

Civil Procedure Code

IPC Sections (for criminal trespass)

⚠️ What NOT to Do

❌ Do not remove encroachment forcefully
❌ Do not threaten or damage property
❌ Do not rely only on verbal agreements

This can backfire legally.

✅ Best Practical Approach (Recommended)

Survey & demarcation

Legal notice

Revenue complaint
